# HOME — What openOODA is

File: guide/Home.oot
User wiki. Canonical URL: https://openooda.org/guide/#Home.oot
Pin: v0.203.0-beta.

## 1. In one minute

openOODA is a systems language. You write `.oo` files.
A systems language talks to the machine.

Effects need a capability token.
A capability token is a permission the function holds.
No token, no effect.

The product path is:

1. Typecheck the program.
2. Emit C.
3. Compile that C with host `gcc`.
4. Run the binary.

That path is `ooda run`.
The same loop is the [C backend](/research/#RP-4-x-backend-c-product-floor.oot).
More on the path: [How it works](Limits.oot).

This page is the start of the user wiki.
Site: https://openooda.org/
Guide: https://openooda.org/guide/
Registry: https://registry.openooda.org
Research library: https://openooda.org/research/

## 2. Start here

Install is `curl -fsSL https://openooda.org/install | oo`.
The payload is `.oo`. Never `| sh`.
See [Start](Use.oot). That page is how you install, check, run, and test a file.

## 3. Hello

Save this as `hello.oo`:

```
pub fn greet(name: String) -> String {
    return "Hello, " + name + "!";
}

verify greet {
    assert_eq!(greet("World"), "Hello, World!");
}

pub fn main() {
    println(greet("openOODA"));
}
```

`pub fn` is a public function.
`verify` is a test block next to the function.

```
ooda check hello.oo
ooda run hello.oo
ooda test hello.oo
```

`ooda run` typechecks, emits C, calls host `gcc`, and runs the binary.
It prints the greeting. It does not run `verify`.
`ooda test` runs `verify` / `assert_eq!` / `assert!`.

First run steps: [Start](Use.oot).
Syntax and types: [Language](Language.oot).

## 4. Commands

You drive the language with `ooda`.
The full flag list lives on [Commands](Options.oot).

| Command | What you use it for |
| :--- | :--- |
| `check` | Typecheck a `.oo` file. |
| `build` | Emit C and compile with host `gcc`. |
| `run` | Typecheck, emit C, `gcc`, run. |
| `test` | Run `verify` / `assert_eq!` / `assert!`. |
| `bench` | Time `oodac check`. |
| `dump` | Print tokens, ast, or check. |
| `outline` | Names and shapes in a file. |
| `reflect` | Look up one symbol. |
| `fix` | Apply compiler patches. |
| `patch` | Replace one function body. |
| `lsp` | Talk to your editor. |
| `context` | List `.oo` files. |
| `digest` | Hash a file. |
| `health` | Check the toolchain. |
| `pkg` | List locked packages. |
| `add` | Install a package from the stock store. |
| `search` | Search every store you added. |
| `registry` | List stores. `add` / `remove` a URL. |
| `pm` | Package manager. |

`outline`, `reflect`, `fix`, and `patch` keep the edit loop small.
That is the [AI-native language](/research/#RP-ES-1-es-ai-native-systems-language.oot) surface.
The name of the loop is [OODA loop speed](/research/#RP-1-1-philosophy-ooda-loop-speed.oot).

## 5. Capabilities

A function that does an effect takes a token, for example `&FsReadCap`.
The token is a permission that function holds.
You pass the token in. You do not hide the effect.

Common tokens: files, process, env, alloc, threads, sys.
Prefer the small token.
`write_file` needs `&FsWriteCap` and `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR`.
Details: [Capabilities](Safety.oot).
Flags and env: [Commands](Options.oot).

The model is [capability sandboxing](/research/#RP-3-1-unified-capability-sandboxing.oot).

## 6. Packages and std

Product std ships as `ooda/std`.
Import from that tree.
Named emit+gcc+run is the proof. A folder name is not a volume cert.

Add a package with `ooda add hello`.
That uses the stock store.
`ooda search` walks every store you added.
`ooda launch --exec NAME` runs a gated payload.
The public catalog is https://registry.openooda.org
How packages work: [Packages](Packages.oot).

The compiler, CLI, tests, and package tools are `.oo`.
That is the [self-hosted ecosystem](/research/#RP-5-1-self-hosted-ecosystem.oot) shape.

